A triple $(h, r, t)$ with $\operatorname{type}(h) = \tau^h$, $\operatorname{type}(t) = \tau^t$ is \emph{type-valid} w.r.t.\ $\mathcal{O}$ iff:
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:type-valid}
  (\tau^h, \tau^t) \in \sigma(r).
\end{equation}
%
This is strictly stronger than checking domain and range independently ($\tau^h \in \operatorname{dom}(r) \wedge \tau^t \in \operatorname{range}(r)$), because most relations have non-rectangular type signatures: $\sigma(r) \subsetneq \operatorname{dom}(r) \times \operatorname{range}(r)$ for all 7 SciERC and 73/96 DocRED relations.\footnote{An independent check would over-permit type pairs by $1.50\times$ on SciERC and $1.58\times$ on DocRED (details in Appendix~\ref{app:discussion}).}
Our goal is a decoding strategy maximizing $P(y \mid x)$ subject to ontology compliance: all triples must be type-valid under $\mathcal{O}$, and $y$ must be well-formed JSON conforming to Eq.~\eqref{eq:output-schema}.

